# Line endings are a repository property, not a per-machine git setting.
#
# This repo is written on Windows and run in Linux containers. `core.autocrlf`
# happens to be true on the author's machine, so the files are already stored
# with LF -- but a clone on a machine without that setting would commit CRLF,
# and GNU make before 4.3 then joins the trailing CR onto the last word of a
# recipe. `$(XLSX)` becoming `../data/B1_Mine.xlsx\r` fails in a way nobody
# enjoys reading.
* text=auto

# These are read by a shell, a container runtime or make, all of which are
# entitled to object to a CR.
Makefile text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Binary, so git neither diffs them nor touches their bytes.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.xlsx binary
